<section id="architecture">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-tag">03 · COMPUTER ARCHITECTURE</div>

    <h2>How the Apollo Guidance Computer Managed a Moon Landing</h2>

    <p class="section-description">
      The Apollo Guidance Computer (AGC) guided and controlled the Lunar Module
      during the Apollo missions. Although it had only a 2.048 MHz processor, 4
      KB of RAM, and 72 KB of ROM, its software architecture allowed it to
      perform reliable real-time navigation under strict hardware limitations.
      Explore the concepts below to see how the AGC managed processor resources,
      prioritized critical tasks, and recovered from overloads.
    </p>

    <Chip client:load />
    <footer>
      The Apollo Guidance Computer achieved reliable real-time control with only
      4 KB of RAM and 72 KB of ROM. Rather than relying on powerful hardware, it
      depended on efficient software architecture, including workload
      management, priority scheduling, real-time execution, program alarms, and
      automatic recovery. These same architectural principles are still used
      today in embedded and real-time systems such as spacecraft, aircraft,
      automobiles, and medical devices.
    </footer>
  </div>
</section>
